of modification was further developed to produce synthetic zeolites by altering the structure between the primary \(\mathrm{(TO_{4},}\) where \(\mathrm{T}=\mathrm{Al}\) or Si) and secondary binding units.Along with such efforts, many new materials, including metal oxides, porous chalcogenides, halides, nitrides, and carbides have also been developed since the 1990s as important adsorbents for the treatment of VOCs [15–18]. Activated carbon has also been widely studied, particularly for the separation and storage of gaseous compounds [19]. This material has been appealing due to its cost effectiveness, high open porosity with high specific surface area, and twisted network of defective hexagonal carbon layers (with pore diameters of approximately \(5\,\mathrm{nm}\) ) [20]. At the same time, activated carbon has a number of drawbacks such as: (a) requirement for special, expensive distillation (or extraction) processes for product recovery, (b) progressive deterioration in capacity with recycling, (c) requirement for a steam or vacuum source for regeneration, (d) requirement for a cooling gas stream to match the usual range of operation temperatures (below \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), and (e) high capital cost. To counter the various challenges of many types of common materials, coordination polymers (CPs) have recently been studied extensively in recognition of their advanced capacities for capture, storage, separation, and catalytic degradation of environmental pollutants, especially VOCs.
More recently, CPs have become commonly known as MOFs that are well defined as crystalline solid materials [21]. Fundamentally, CPs/MOFs are novel, porous materials that can be used in a wide range of applications due to their excellent features including low framework density, crystallized nano \((<\!2\,\mathrm{nm})\) or meso \((2{-}50\,\mathrm{nm})\) porous structures, breathability, and open metal sites for interactions [22–24]. To date, numerous ligands have been tested as connectors with the metal ion nodes based on the laws of assembly and effective synthetic strategies such as "node-and-spacer" [25], secondary building units [26, 27], and tertiary building units [28]. In addition, the physicochemical properties of the resulting products have been verified using theoretical molecular modeling [29–31] as well as experimental methodologies (e.g., separation [32], sorption [33, 34], and catalysis [35–39]).
More importantly, synthesis methods for CPs/MOFs have been developed through careful selection or evaluation of the morphology of traditional materials along with their elemental compositions. For instance, a number of synthetic approaches (e.g., hydrothermal, solvothermal, sonochemical, and microwave-assisted methods) have been developed or modified based on the approaches initially employed for zeolites (Table 1). The basic principles of hydrothermal and solvothermal synthesis methods are comparable, with the exception of some differences in operating conditions. For instance, the former needs sophisticated instruments such as autoclaves to achieve elevated temperature and pressure conditions, while the latter can be carried out under ambient conditions (e.g., with simpler analytical equipment). In contrast, sol-gel and surfactant-assisted synthesis methods were developed based on the synthesis of metal oxides. In general, sol-gel methods can yield materials with more uniform pores than those achieved using sonochemical or surfactant
methods. As of now, microwave-assisted synthesis requires the shortest reaction time to prepare CPs/ MOFs. In this case, the applied voltage directly interacts with the reactant molecules to enhance the heat of diffusion in the reaction medium (Table 1). On the other hand, active charges on MOF surfaces have been achieved using surfactant-assisted synthesis. Moreover, surface chemists, physicists, and material chemists continue to introduce many new approaches for the synthesis of MOFs such as electrochemical and mechanochemical synthesis. The development of these new approaches has helped to expand the potential applications of MOFs to the field of separation owing to the addition of magnetic properties [40, 41].
In this review, we focus mainly on the potent role that porous MOFs have in controlling airborne pollutants, with an emphasis on VOCs, through sorption and catalytic approaches. The adsorbate–adsorbent relationship is accounted for in relation to the physical parameters of VOCs (e.g., polarity
and dipole moment) and the pore features of MOFs (e.g., channel or cage size, shape, crystal geometry, pore cavities, and open metal sites). The adsorption mechanisms of VOCs are also assessed based on experimental and simulated results as a function of the crystallographic positions, orientation of the guest molecules, and level of adsorption with pressure and temperature variation. In addition, the importance of the structural characteristics (e.g., ligand length, suitable solvent, and postsynthetic template assistance) is also examined. Moreover, some of the drawbacks of MOFs when compared with traditional and commercial materials are also evaluated in terms of their capacity, efficiency, recyclability (or environmental friendliness), and cost effectiveness. To accomplish this, we have relied on the classification of CPs/MOFs and their synthesis methods to meaningfully account for their application toward the abatement of VOCs. The underlying capacity of CPs/MOFs is also evaluated with the aim of expanding their technical advancements for air quality management (AQM), especially for VOCs. The state-of-the-art performance of CPs/ MOFs for controlling VOCs has also been described by investigating a number of recent case studies. The scope of this review is extended further to help establish an appropriate future roadmap for the control and management of VOCs.
## 2 Features of CPs/MOFs for capture and degradation of VOCs
To date, the capture and storage of VOCs is a challenging task owing to the high reactivity of their functional moieties. For instance, as some VOCs (e.g., those with oxygenated and aminated groups) undergo photochemical reactions, they can negatively influence air quality; e.g., by the active formation of smoke [42]. The presence of strong (coordination bonds) and/or weak bonds (\(\pi-\pi\) interactions, hydrogen bonding, and van der Waals forces) in CPs/MOFs is critical for determining their structural polarity [43]. Therefore, material chemists have utilized the infinite crystalline properties of MOFs to effectively fabricate VOCs inside their cages and/or windows using so-called host–guest interactions (Fig. 1).
(a) Based on the structure
Although the intriguing properties of CPs/MOFs can be controlled efficiently during the synthesis procedure, many difficulties are still encountered in real scenarios, which can mainly be ascribed to factors associated with the counterions, templates, reaction temperature, time, pH, and solvent medium [45–47]. More recently, these issues have generally been resolved by the use of multitopic bridging ligands [45, 48–50]. The multidentate ligands are commonly carboxylate ligands with two or more functional
moieties (e.g., nitrogen and sulfur atoms) [48]. This multifunctionality can help the equilibration of external forces that arise during synthesis, treatment, and application [51].
To select the optimum MOF for the treatment of VOCs, it is important to select an experimental technique to accurately determine the adsorption capacity, isosteric heat, and desorption capacity (Table 2). For instance, assessing the gas adsorption isotherm at a fixed pressure is a commonly adopted approach for evaluating the limiting adsorption capacity of MOFs for VOCs. For example, the difference between the pressure at the adsorption equilibrium and the initial vapor pressure (adsorption capacity vs. \(P/P^{\circ}\)) can be used to calculate the limiting adsorption capacity [52]. However, relatively little is known about
the adsorption behavior of VOCs with respect to the temperature swing process (TSP) under low pressure conditions [53]. Volumetric (or manometric) and gravimetric methods have also been recommended as techniques for evaluating adsorption. The former method is based on the mass balance of the amount of gas adsorbed on the sorbent material. This is mainly used in open systems using a carrier gas (such as \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)) for single-component analysis. For multicomponent analysis, a gas chromatograph (GC) is added along with a closed system [54]. The adsorbed amount can then be calculated by determining the difference in the total amount of gas supplied to the sample cell with the adsorbent and the amount of gas in the free space. For application of this approach, it is necessary to determine the void volume of CPs/MOFs before
conducting the adsorption experiment; hence, inert gases, such as helium, are introduced prior to the adsorption experiment (at ambient and adsorption temperatures) to accurately measure the void volume [52].
As an alternative, gravimetric methods can be performed by weighing the sorbent mass that interacts with the gaseous components using a very sensitive microbalance near ambient temperatures. For the gravimetric method, buoyancy corrections are necessary to avoid overestimating the adsorption capacity values [70]. In this case, using a densimetric–gravimetric method (based on directly measuring the gas densities) has been recommended [54]. Oscillometric measurements of gas solubilities in swelling adsorbent materials can also be used to calculate the rotational oscillations of sorbent materials [70]. This phenomenon can be used to reliably study the configuration of the interior structure (e.g., flexibility, rigidity, and breathing effect) of the frameworks. In addition, other possible techniques, such as microcalorimetry, nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, interference microscopy, and mass spectrometry, are also available.
## 3 Mechanism for controlling VOCs using CPs/MOFs
In this section, some important mechanisms, including sorptive removal and catalytic degradation, for the control of VOCs using CPs/MOFs are discussed based on recent investigations made in this area. Furthermore,
modeling and simulation studies in this emerging area are also discussed.
## 3.1 Sorptive removal of VOCs
Typically, adsorptive separation of VOCs relies on the combined effects of the following factors or mechanisms: 1) pore-filling, 2) \(\pi\)-complexation, 3) van der Waals attraction, 4) hydrogen bonding, 5) breathing and/or gate opening effects, and 6) structural properties of the adsorbent. Accordingly, pore-filling is classified as a steric mechanism and is a function of the size and shape exclusion or molecular sieving effect of the adsorbent [74]. In contrast, whereas adsorption due to van der Waals forces generally increases with increasing surface area [75]. Moreover, \(\pi\)-complexation mechanisms are only observed for a few transition metal ions such as \(\mathrm{Cu}\), \(\mathrm{Ag}\), and \(\mathrm{Cr}\) ions [76]. The mechanism based on hydrogen bonding is a special phenomenon that is associated with the dipole moment difference between the electron-rich framework ends (N of \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups) and the electron-deficient adsorbate end \((\mathrm{H}^{+})\); this attractive force tends to decrease when the surface area of the MOF increases [57]. Breathing [77] and/or gate opening [78] can be classified as a kinetic and/or host–guest interaction mechanism. These processes may also be dictated by the difference in the binding energies (isosteric heat \((\Delta H_{\mathrm{s}})\)) of the adsorbate on the adsorbent. In this review, the sorptive removal of VOCs is accounted for by one of the above mechanisms. Table 3 summarizes the sorptive removal of VOCs under certain conditions.
## 3.1.1 Steric mechanism (pore-filling mechanism)
The structure-associated pore-filling mechanism has been discussed most commonly in the recent literature [71, 74, 79, 82, 83, 97]. In this MOF series, MIL-101 frameworks are the most studied owing to their large cell volume, large surface area, numerous unsaturated metal sites (up to \(3.0\;\mathrm{mmol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)), and two types of mesoporous cages (29 and \(34\,\mathrm{\AA}\) in diameter) with microporous windows (12 and \(16\,\mathrm{\AA}\) in diameter) [86]. MIL-101 showed superior adsorption of benzene at \(288\,\mathrm{K}\) \((1,304\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) compared with other porous adsorbents like zeolites and activated carbon [79]. The favorable adsorption behavior of MIL-101 for benzene is caused by pores with the extra-large surfaces \((3,054{-}3,900\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). These extra-large surfaces were obtained by controlling the crystallization, dimensionality of the inorganic sub-networks, and condensation rates of inorganic chromium clusters through the synthesis temperature [98–100]. The binding speed of the chromium clusters with respect to the terephthalate units is affected by the nucleation rate of the product [86]. As such, MIL-53 required a longer induction period owing to the slow nucleation of chromium salt and terephthalic acid in contrast with the
rapid induction period of MIL-101. This fast nucleation of MIL-101 should lead to the formation of highly porous materials for the effective capture of benzene.
In addition, it is important to address the temperature-dependent physisorption (i.e., a decrease in the adsorption capacity with an increase in temperature) and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions of benzene rings between guests and linkers [84], which are also common mechanisms applicable to the capture of VOCs. Because many VOCs are long chain hydrocarbon molecules, the effect of chain length is unavoidable during structural interactions between VOCs and coordination bonds. It has been determined that an increase in the length of the alkane chain (\(\mathrm{C}_{5}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{8}\)) can lead to an extended adsorption equilibration time. This extended adsorption is related to geometric effects of the frameworks, i.e., the confinement packing of VOCs into the pores and cavities of the framework. In contrast, \(\mathrm{C}_{9}\) alkanes have shown significantly different behavior, which is believed to reflect the conformational changes that prevent the filling of \(\mathrm{C}_{9}\) alkanes in MIL frameworks, even in hexagonal windows (\(\sim\!16\,\mathrm{\AA}\)) [71]. In addition, the adsorption of \(\mathrm{C}_{9}\) alkanes was highly influenced by kinetic factors, such as the creation of more a conformational structure,
than that of the lower alkanes. This frequent production of conformers resulted in longer relaxation times for the frameworks or slower diffusion of the molecules into the cages or windows [71].
Steric hindrance is influenced by the size, shape, and kinetic diameter of the adsorbate [74]. An investigation of seven individual VOCs (acetone, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and three xylene isomers) over MIL-101 indicated that the adsorption capacity depends on the
length of the diagonal line of the molecular rectangle plane [74]. Generally, many target VOCs (such as acetone, toluene, benzene, and ethylbenzene) enter into pores with a minimum diameter plane \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{YZ}}\) and \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{XY}}\), whereas other VOCs (e.g., o-xylene and m-xylene) tend to require a maximum axis plane \(\left(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{XZ}}\right)\) (Fig. 2(a)). This phenomenon observed for xylenes was accounted for by their favorable specific interactions with the windows in the small cavities of the MIL-101
