The cages and windows of the MOF play a potent role in the adsorption of gaseous molecules. In this case, the size of the molecular rectangular plane of the guest molecule decides the favorable adsorption interactions of incoming molecules with the framework. For instance, the sorption isotherm of MIL-101 exhibited a better affinity for benzene \(\left(\Delta H = -68\,\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\right)\) than n-hexane \(\left(\Delta H = -61\,\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\right)\). Even in binary mixtures, benzene was specifically adsorbed in tetrahedral sites, whereas n-hexane interacted at pentagonal windows. Therefore, the influence of n-hexane inhibited the stacking interaction of benzene at high coverage (Fig. 2(c)) [80].
A good example of the inverse relationship between the pore-filling mechanism and the cross-sectional area of the VOCs is shown by MOF-177 [82]. Ethylbenzene, xylenes, and ethenylbenzene (molecular size: 0.375– \(0.379\,\mathrm{nm}^{2}\)) showed lower adsorption capacities (213– \(271\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\)) than benzene \(\left(800\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\right)\). This observation suggests that pore filling is favored for compounds with smaller cross-sectional areas such as benzene
\((0.34\,\mathrm{nm}^{2})\). In contrast, in the same study, acetone (cross-section area: \(0.269\,\mathrm{nm}^{2}\)) showed a much lower absorption capacity due to its strong polarity, which prohibited \(\pi{-}\pi\) coupling between the ligands and acetone (Fig. 2). It should be noted that MIL-101 and MOF-177 exhibit very different behaviors for the adsorption of acetone [74, 82]. As shown in Fig. 3, MIL-101 reduced the maximum adsorption capacity for acetone owing to its minimal molecular axis plane. In contrast, MOF-177 showed a smaller adsorption capacity due to the polar nature of acetone (with a similar pore-filling mechanism). This variation in behavior was ascribed to the construction of the framework, which had differences in the metal centers and linking units. Likewise, the pore-filling mechanism can be controlled by adjusting the nature and size of the pores in the framework.
## 3.1.2 Unsaturated metal sites in the framework
The availability of unsaturated metal sites in the framework leads to \(\pi\)-complexation through electron donor–acceptor mechanisms [61, 91, 96]. Generally, the electron-deficient metal centers can act as Lewis acids, but they can also behave as Lewis bases and attract polar compounds.
To date, MOF-199 (Cu-BTC or HKUST-1) is one of the most studied open-metal-site MOFs. The adsorption of VOCs over such frameworks is mainly dictated by \(\pi\)-complexation mechanisms [61, 91, 96]. As summarized in Table 3, Cu-MOFs exhibit considerably high adsorption capacities for the most common target pollutants such as benzene [61, 91, 96], toluene [90, 96],
propylene [77], and isobutene [83]. Cu-BTC is a well-established MOF, and its production is favored by industry (Fig. 4(a)). Hence, in many studies, it has been recommended as a substitute for traditional materials (Fig. 4(b)). Cu-BTC is well-known for its high adsorption capacity along with colorimetric indication [96]. Thus, this material has been applied for the large-scale treatment of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in flue gas mixtures (Fig. 4(c)).
To extend the applicability of such approaches to VOCs, the sorptive removal of propane, propylene, and isobutene was studied using Cu-BTC extrudates (manufactured by Sigma–Aldrich) and its performance was compared relative to the powder form [89]. The adsorption of propylene occurred preferably due to the interaction between the electron-rich \(\pi\) -bonding orbitals in propylene molecules and the vacant s-orbitals of Cu atoms in the Cu-BTC lattice (Fig. 4(d)) [77, 83, 104]. The favorable absorption can also be recognized by examining the \(\Delta H_{s}\) values, i.e., the
surface loading limits for the sorption capacity were observed to decrease (Fig. 4(e)) [77]. In addition, the extrudate form showed a \(63\%\) loss in sorption capacity in the high-pressure region \((100\;\mathrm{kPa})\) relative to the powder form. This phenomenon suggests a possible reduction in the strength of crystallinity during extrusion processes [89]. As mentioned earlier, the polarity of the VOCs plays an important role in the sorption process. For instance, the Gibb's free energy \((\Delta G_{\mathrm{ad}})\) values of MOF-5 for the adsorption of toluene and phenol are \(-4.0\) and \(-10.2\;\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\), respectively [105]. The observed differences in the \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{ad}}\) values can be explained by the non-polar nature of the \(\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\) group (in toluene), which reduces \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{ad}}\) relative to the polar nature of the \(-\mathrm{OH}\) group (in phenol). Furthermore, open metal sites in MOFs (e.g., MOF-199) can have dual advantages, depending on the polarity of the VOCs involved in the sorption process. As such, highly polar compounds generate an additional bond between the incoming VOC and a Cu-ion [105]. This
change in the chemical environment led to an almost two-fold difference in \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{ad}}\) between polar and nonpolar VOCs (e.g., \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{ad}}\) of toluene: \(39.9\,\mathrm{kJ{\cdot}mol^{-1}}\) and \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{ad}}\) of phenol/indole: \(-53.9\,\mathrm{kJ{\cdot}mol^{-1}}\)).
MIL-101 is another open site MOF that has been widely investigated. Huang et al. [70] studied the adsorption of several polar and non-polar VOCs (n-hexane, toluene, methanol, butanone, dichloromethane, and n-butylamine) by MIL-101. The recorded adsorption capacity was in the order of n-hexane \(<\) toluene \(<\) butanone \(<\) dichloromethane \(<\) methanol \(<\) n-butylamine (Table 3). This observation indicates the existence of interactions between unsaturated metal sites (electron acceptors) and the heteroatoms of VOCs (electron donors). Generally, unsaturated metal sites favor polar molecules with higher dipole moments (e.g., acetone) over weakly polar molecules (e.g., p-xylene) [85]. In addition, the adsorption of p-xylene molecules in the low-pressure region was attributed to the free apertures \((<\!12\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) rather than to the mesoporous cages of the MIL-101 framework [106]. Hence, the surface properties of the MIL-101 framework
need to be clearly characterized. In this respect, the surface properties of MIL-101 were analyzed using the inverse gas chromatography (IGC) technique with three polar probes (acetone, toluene, and p-xylene) at \(453\;\mathrm{K}\) (Fig. 5(a)) [85]. p-Xylene is a weakly polar compound with the lowest specific Gibbs free energy \((\Delta G_{\mathrm{a}}^{s}\,=\,-1.69\,\mathrm{\kJ{\cdot}mol^{-1}})\), whereas acetone is a polar compound with a relatively large Gibbs free energy \((\Delta G_{\mathrm{a}}^{s}\,=\,-3.77\,\mathrm{\kJ{\cdot}mol^{-1}})\). This comparison indicated a favorable interaction between acetone and the unsaturated metal sites of MIL-101, which led to an enhanced adsorption capacity of acetone [85].
The availability of free electrons in the metal sites allows this platform to be used to improve the surface characteristics via ion exchange. Synthesized Cu-BTC, when loaded with \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\)and polyoxometalates (POM), demonstrates enhanced sorption capacity for benzene (Cu-BTC \((251\;\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\)), and Cu-BTC loaded with \(3\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\,(257\,\mathsf{m g}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\), and Cu-BTC loaded with \(28\;\mathrm{\wt.}\%\) \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\,(264\;\mathsf{m g}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\)). Essentially, these enhancements were related to \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions between the ligands in the framework and benzene [69].
MIL-101 frameworks can also be used to study ion exchange properties because they possess \(\sim3.0\,\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) of open \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) ions. The sorption capacities of the parent MIL-101 and different loadings (1 wt.%, 3 wt.%, and 5 wt.%) of \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\) were investigated [87]. As shown in Fig. 5(b), the measured toluene adsorption isotherm at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) in the high-pressure region \((P/P^{\circ} = 0{-}1)\) yielded adsorption capacities of 1,110, 1,208, 1,285, and \(1,229\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) for (1) MIL-101, (2) \(1\%\) , (3) \(3\%\) , and (4) \(5\%\) , \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\) , respectively. In contrast, in the low-pressure region \((P/P^{\circ}=0{-}0.1)\) , the toluene adsorption capacity was \(450\%\) higher for 3 wt. \(\%\) \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\) compared with that of unmodified MIL-101 [69, 87]. This observation implies that MIL-101 should also be a suitable candidate for surface modification, similar to Cu-BTC under low-pressure conditions [90]. Note that the removal of indoor air pollutants can proceed in the pressure range of \(P/P^{\circ}=0{-}0.1\).
In another case, the adsorption isotherm of n-hexane was investigated using MIL-101 loaded with 5 wt. \(\%\) and \(10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) graphite oxide (GO) [88]. The observed results were comparable to the study of MIL-101 with \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\) i.e., the maximum capacity was achieved at the optimum loading (e.g., 3 wt. \(\%\) \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}/\) MIL-101 and \(5\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) \(\mathrm{MIL@GO)}\) via the dominant interactions of \(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\) (or GO) with \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\). Here, due to the interactive competition between metal ions for the linker (benzene dicarboxylic acid (BDC)), the metal center was left with more unsaturated \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) [107]. However, at higher loadings (5 wt. \(\%\) of \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\) and \(10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) of GO), the pore volume and surface area were diminished by the accommodation of excess quantities of both exchange ions and BDC linkers. Hence, using ion exchange of the open metal sites to enhance the sorption capacity is highly dependent on the ratio of loaded molecules (e.g., \(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\)) and available linker (e.g., BDC).
## 3.1.3 Hydrogen bonding
In the literature, only a few case studies have attempted to explain the hydrogen bonding mechanism. Instead, most studies have focused on adsorption in the liquid phase. For example, there are many reports on p-nitrophenol adsorption over \(\mathrm{MIL-101-NH}_{2}\) (where the nitro group interacts with the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101 framework) [108], ammonia over MOF-GO (where H in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) group interacts with the tetrahedral zinc oxide unit) [109], and indole over \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 (where H in the indole group interacts with N in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 framework) [109].
oxide unit) [109], and indole over \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 (where H in the indole group interacts with N in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 framework) [109].
To the best of our knowledge, there have been only two reports that have meaningfully dealt the hydrogen bonding mechanism of pollutants in the gaseous phase [57, 110]. The hydrogen bonding mechanism can be used to explain increases in adsorption with decreases in surface area (or increases in temperature). Because in the physisorption process, an increase in temperature decreases the surface interactions, most molecules are repulsed by the surface barrier (bottle neck effect). In other words, vapor-phase adsorption over a clean surface is an exothermic process. For instance, the hydrogen bonding mechanism was proposed for pyridine adsorption over \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) based on the physicochemical properties of the adsorbent, e.g., temperature and functional group [57] (Figs. 6(a) and 6(b)). The amount of adsorbed pyridine increased as the temperature increased; however, it decreased as the surface area increased (Fig. 6(b)). This observation suggests the absence of electrostatic or van der Waals interactions [95, 111]. In addition, the absence of base–base repulsion between the N group of pyridine and the N group of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) can be established by calculating the enthalpy, with an estimated binding energy of about \(-74.2\,\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\) (Figs. 6(c) and 6(d)) [57, 112–114].
The type of functional group present in the clusters of the MOF plays an important role in capturing gaseous components. In this respect, structural modification of the parent MOF with a suitable co-catalyst may help increase the sorption capacity through one of the above mentioned mechanisms. Hence, although the parent MIL-101 framework is known to exhibit an electron donor–acceptor mechanism, it favors a hydrogen bonding mechanism between the O atom of POM and the alcohol group of the adsorbate (e.g., methanol, ethanol, and propanol) upon exchange with POM (Table 3) [69].
In general, during gas sorption, charged ions in the framework are expected to help attract gaseous molecules near the framework clusters through so-called ion-electrostatic interactions. In this mechanism, the fully charged clusters of the framework induce electrostatic interactions between the dipoles of the temporarily charged guest molecules. Commonly, most
of MOFs are not charged (with the exception of ligand functional groups); therefore, surface charges are created by co-catalysts or doping agents. For instance, the NENU framework was doped with \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\) ions for 3 and 28 days to form NENU-3 and NENU-28, respectively [69]. When the \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\)-loaded phases were utilized for the adsorption of methanol, their capacities were measured as 189 and \(215\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) (at \(P/P^{\circ}=0.2\)), respectively. The slightly enhanced adsorption of NENU-28 (exchanged with both \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\) and POM) was induced by ion–dipole electrostatic interactions. The larger radius of \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\) (cations) provides a stronger dipole interaction with the partially negative ends of the vapor molecules. Therefore, the inclusion of alkali metal cations can help increase the sorption capacity through either hydrogen bonding or ion–dipole interactions; this was in fact confirmed by enthalpy calculations at 298 K (\(\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\); NENU-28 = 43.7 kJ·mol\(^{-1}\), NENU-3 = 37.6 kJ·mol\(^{-1}\)) [69].
## 3.1.4 Breathing and gate opening effect
Structural properties are important because they can control the host–guest interactions during the adsorption process. Therefore, the adsorption isotherms
of light hydrocarbons were evaluated using experimental and molecular simulations with both flexible MIL-53(Cr) and rigid MIL-47(V) [115]. A close relationship between the adsorption enthalpies and the carbon number (or polarizability) of the alkanes was revealed, with increases in the carbon chain length inducing increases in the enthalpy value (\(\sim8.25\,\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\)) of adsorption [115]. This observation may be ascribable to the formation of van der Waals bonds upon the addition of each \(\mathrm{-CH}_{2}\) group (Fig. 7(a)). In this series, the breathing property of the framework is also an important parameter. Such parameters can be used to explain the dramatic increases or decreases in the cell volume of the frameworks, which occur without a loss of crystallinity or bond breaking, during the adsorption process [116]. This phenomenon encourages host–guest interactions and adequate fitting within the pore size. As such, the uptake of lighter alkanes can be increased by about two-fold [117]. Conceptually, breathing is determined by the change in the adsorption enthalpy values (\(\Delta H\)); however, this is not true for all species. For instance, the relative affinities of the sorbate over large (lp) and narrow pores (np) are favored by the double transitions from lp to np and np to lp, depending
on the relative guest affinities (Fig. 7(b)) [118].
Along with the MIL series, nanoporous MOF-5 has also shown the breathing mechanism during the adsorption of benzene molecules [92]. Nanoporous or breathing materials can exhibit contrasting patterns in their adsorption isotherms (Fig. 7(c)), indicating the occurrence of the breathing phenomenon at low-pressure loading conditions. The observation of two pressure range properties was also confirmed by analyzing the adsorption energy distribution (AED) peaks. Accordingly, the low-energy peak was nearly 7.7 times larger than the high-energy peak, which directly indicated the breathing phenomenon in MOF-5 (Fig. 7(d)) [92].
Under certain circumstances, the combined effects of high surface area and the nature of the framework (e.g., harder or softer) play an important role in applications at elevated temperatures [78, 81, 119, 120].
Commonly, cracking long-chain hydrocarbons requires elevated pressures due to insufficient energy penalties for adsorption and separation measurements. Among the various mechanisms, gate opening is vital for the separation of olefin/paraffin mixtures (Fig. 8(a)) [78]. In this respect, ZIF-7 is a good example; its hydrocarbon adsorption is minimal (due to insufficient opening of the pores) at low pressures. However, at elevated pressures, uniform nanopore cavities (0.3 nm) are opened and filled owing to adsorption inside the cavities (Fig. 8(b)) [78, 121]. This abrupt orientation change likely favors efficient desorption due to the interaction energies of the linker and the adsorbate in the low pressure range (Fig. 8(c)) [78].
## 3.1.5 Structural properties
The structural properties of the framework provide some insight into the adsorption mechanism. The
presence of amino (IRMOF-3) or alkyne (IRMOF-62) functionalities in the framework can help increase the sorption capacity of MOFs [96]. In addition, rigidity (MIL-53) and structural flexibility (MIL-47) can also facilitate the separation of olefin/paraffin compounds [115]. In addition, the homogeneous and heterogeneous arrangement of ligands on the framework can alter the adsorption properties for polar and non-polar compounds [94]. For instance, the significance of the structure was examined for MOFs with different topologies (MOF-5 and monoclinic MOF-5) for the adsorptive separation of xylene isomers and ethylbenzene [61]. MOF-5 was shown to preferably adsorb p-xylene and \(\alpha\)-xylene in the low-pressure region (Fig. 9(a)); however, the monoclinic topology showed superior adsorption in the high-pressure region (Fig. 9(b)). When the polarity of these MOFs was analyzed in terms of the McReynolds constant, MOF-5 exhibited more nonpolar properties than monoclinic MOF-5 [122, 123]. This behavior opens up the opportunity to utilize MOF-5 as a non-polar stationary phase in GC columns to separate xylene isomers (Fig. 9(c)) [94].
## 3.1.6 Adsorbate characteristics
VOCs represent a wide variety of organic compounds with different physical and chemical properties. The special physical characteristics of VOCs (e.g., polar, non-polar, and inert) are beneficial in the field of adsorptive separation. For example, the inert nature of cyclohexane prohibits adsorption upon Cu-BTC; thus, this MOF can be used to help diminish the sorption capacity of cyclohexane (1.48 mg·g\(^{-1}\) at \(P/P^{\circ}=0.2\)) [69]. As another example, the adsorption of isobutene was restricted due to its low affinity on the surface of Cu-BTC, owing to an insignificant number of binding sites on the surface of the framework [77]. As such, it appears to be very important to describe the adsorbate characteristics during the sorptive removal analysis of VOCs.
## 3.2 Catalytic degradation
Generally, the advanced oxidative process (AOP) is proposed as an effective tool for the destruction of a wide variety of VOCs. This approach is known to facilitate the generation of reactive radicals that attack
C–C, C–H, C–N, C–S, or C–P bonds [124]. Essentially, Fenton reactions, which occur through hydroxyl radicals, are the most popular due to their availability in AOPs. However, catalytic degradation of harmful VOCs into non-toxic substances should be regarded as the ideal goal for abatement processes because this can suppress or eliminate adsorbent saturation and/or the occurrence of secondary emission [125, 126].
The development of catalytic degradation, especially by using heterogeneous catalysts, is one of the most promising areas in current CPs/MOFs research. This is because the catalytic properties of these porous materials can be finely tuned [126, 127]. The catalytic degradation of VOCs by CPs/MOFs is based on several processes, including (a) embedding active sites in cavities, (b) using post-synthetic methods, and (c) incorporation (or encapsulation) of guest species (such as functional groups, metal nanoparticles, and other guest molecules) [125, 126]. In addition, many excellent features of MOFs (e.g., controlled fine structure, high crystallinity, and theoretical limitation of pore size) have been recognized as useful for the catalytic
treatment of VOCs. However, to date, there have only been a few reports discussing the catalytic activity of CPs/MOFs for such treatment purposes [101, 125, 128]. For example, rigid nanoporous CPs/MOFs (e.g., MIL101) with large pores and high surface areas have shown great structural resistance to air, water, and common solvents during the thermal treatment of VOCs. Interestingly, CPs/MOFs were able to immobilize Keggin heteropolytungstate within the cages of the framework under thermal treatment conditions due to the nature of the pores [101].
Recently, the performance of Zr-based MOFs was investigated for the selective oxidation of cyclooctene into cyclooctene oxide [129]. The catalytic conversion ability (\%) of Zr-MOF-(bpy)-\(\mathrm{CuBr}_{2}\) was superior (88.5% with 95.3% selectivity) to that of the corresponding homogeneous catalyst (e.g., (bpy)-\(\mathrm{CuBr}_{2}\): 28.5% with 74.4% selectivity). According to diffuse reflectance UV–Vis and X-ray absorption line structure (XAFS) measurements, the immobilized Cu species, which coordinated two N atoms and two Br atoms in a square-planar geometry, offered high activity and
selectivity toward the oxidation of cyclooctene into cyclooctene oxide in the presence of tert-butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) (Fig. 10) [129]. Moreover, for the treatment of toluene, a hydrophobic synthetic zeolite (ZSM-5) was used as an adsorbent/catalyst in a promising hybrid process [130]. The experimental results showed that toluene was adsorbed efficiently on ZSM-5 to yield mainly \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) (plus some by-products such as acetic acid and acetaldehyde). These also remained adsorbed after oxidative regeneration with ozone (Fig. 11). Finally, when tested after four consecutive adsorption/ozonation cycles, the adsorption efficiency of ZSM-5 remained stable within \( 92\%-99\% \) [130].
## 3.3 Modeling and simulations
It is highly desirable to properly design and/or simulate
the interactions between solid adsorbents and VOCs (Table 4). This is especially important for the treatment of hazardous gases [70]. The most commonly used simulation to derive adsorption isotherms and isosteric heats of adsorption is the Grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) method [52]. Because many researchers have already focused on simulation methods based on GCMC, here we focus on the theoretical isotherm models used for a variety of calculations (e.g., adsorption capacity, enthalpy, and diffusion constants in single- and multicomponent analyses). Generally, for single-component analysis, the use of the Langmuir model [55], Dubinin–Astakhov equation [70], and UNILAN equation [56] are reliable. In the case of multicomponent analysis, a number of models (Freundlich isotherm [55], Toth equation [55], and dual-site Langmuir model [91]) have been recommend as suitable because they can be used to express submonolayer coverage and the separation energy or diffusion content of two phases on one solid surface. To analyze nanoporous materials, the hybrid Langmuir–Sips model [91] is reliable; this model provides insight into the surface adsorption and capillary condensation results. Interaction energy calculations have also been carried out to adopt the density function theory (DFT) simulation [57], which provides information regarding the surface charge details of each atom on the surface.
## 4 Challenges in MOF technology for the sorption of VOCs
## 4.1 Activation processes
MOFs have received a lot of attention because of their large porosity and the ability to easily tune their pore shape and size from the microporous to the mesoporous scale. Such tuning has been accomplished by changing the connectivity of the inorganic moiety and the nature of the organic linkers [20, 24, 131]. For example, Cu-BTC (MOF-199) is one of the most commonly used MOFs for the adsorption of \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S} \) due to its outstanding reactive adsorption properties, which result from the formation of copper sulfide, as well as the high surface area and pore volume [132–134]. Furthermore, the open and extra coordination ability of metal sites in MOF-199 makes it an excellent
adsorbent or molecular separator of pollutants.
To unitize the metal sites of MOFs, many activation strategies, including (a) thermal activation (TA), (b) solvent exchange, (c) freeze-drying, (d) supercritical \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) exchange, and (e) chemical treatment, have been applied as prerequisite steps for target applications [135–137]. Among these strategies, TA has been the most common option due to its adaptability for both coordinated and pore-filling solvents. However, TA often has a negative influence on the chemical structural integrity of MOFs [136]. It has since been determined that the structural damage caused by TA can be recovered or minimized by utilizing chloroform \( \left(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\right) \) [44]. It is interesting to note that solvents with relatively low boiling points (e.g., \( \mathrm{CHCl}_{3} \) and methylene chloride \( \left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right) \)) are frequently utilized in solvent exchange processes to maintain permanent porosity by removing pore-filling solvents [44, 135, 137]. Hopefully, in the future, the performance of activated/functionalized MOFs will be evaluated as specific molecular probes/sensing materials for the treatment of gaseous VOCs.
## 4.2 Reversibility and structural stability of MOFs
Based on our comprehensive review of the literature,
the ability to adjust the structural stability (i.e., framework, thermal, and chemical stability) has emerged as a very effective tool for practical applications of MOFs, especially toward the capture and degradation of VOCs. In this section, we discuss the relationship between structure stability and reversibility of the MOF, along with their associated possible applications. Basically, structural stability refers to whether an MOF structure is preserved or collapses due to the removal of guest molecules, changes in environmental conditions, and external sources of activation/stimulation [44]. For example, one of the most widely studied MOFs (MOF-5) has a large pore volume and large surface area. However, its practical applications are often limited due to its structural instability under ambient conditions and in aqueous media [138].
To date, several strategies have been used to enhance the structural stability of MOFs after adsorption of hazardous gaseous pollutants (e.g., using metal clusters, such as metal organic polyhedra, as nodes and supramolecular building units). Along with the structural flexibility of MOFs, their dynamic natures are also found to follow a different set of rules than their stability. The reversibility of MOFs has been widely studied through temperature or pressure swing
adsorption processes. In this respect, after saturation of an MOF with target vapor, the MOF was degassed under high-pressure or vacuum conditions and subjected to an adsorption experiment (Fig. 12). The parent MOFs doped with co-catalysts, such as metal ions \(\left(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\right)\) or heterogeneous materials (GO), exhibited comparatively high or similar desorption characteristics up to five adsorption–desorption cycles [87, 88, 129]. For instance, MIL-101@GO composites showed \(\sim800\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) adsorption of n-hexane for five consecutive adsorption–desorption cycles by outgassing the sorbed material at 0.07 mbar and 298 K [88]. Acetone adsorption over \(\mathrm{GrO@MIL-101}\) was also highly reversible under desorption at 0.04 mbar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\). The desorption efficiency obtained for acetone after the sixth cycle was \(91.3\%\), with a constant adsorption capacity of \(1,068\;\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) [139].
In most cases, the original frameworks of the MOFs tend to shrink after the guest molecules are removed or when the material undergoes external activation/stimulation, which is accompanied by a crystal-to-crystal or crystal-to-amorphous transformation [140]. The occurrence of structural reduction can lead to the collapse of the main framework and breakage of the coordination bonds. As such, a lack of stability in aqueous media is of large concern when using MOFs, with respect to their ability to capture and degrade VOCs in aqueous media [141]. In most studies, characterization of MOFs after exposure to water allows the relevant structural factors to be determined. However, as we are interested in gas sorption, we
narrowed down our discussion toward the aqueous sorption.
## 4.3 Environmental impact
The limited durability and high cost of synthesis have limited the application of MOFs in many respects. Therefore, it remains questionable whether it would be more beneficial to synthesize new MOFs with inexpensive materials or to develop low-cost synthesis methods for large-scale applications. In addition, the regeneration of MOFs via a process with low environmental impact is another option for achieving inexpensive and efficient synthesis on a bulk-scale. In addition, MOF precursors can be easily recovered from degraded or deteriorated materials. It is very important to establish such regeneration or recycling steps in MOF synthesis.
Recently, many researchers have investigated methods to expand the feasibility of utilizing MOFs in real-world applications. For example, Han and Lah [142] investigated the simple and efficient regeneration of MOF-5 and HKUST-1 through acid–base treatment. Interestingly, regeneration protocols are environmentally benign and cost-effective owing to the reactants used on deteriorated MOFs. Principally, pristine MOF-5 and HKUST-1 are regenerated at high yields by adjusting the solution pH. This is done by using either in situ base formation or ex situ base addition. Here, the reactants are recovered by simply dissolving the deteriorated MOFs in a strong acid (Fig. 13) [142]. Importantly, the ex situ base addition
protocol is predicated on a very inexpensive and environmentally friendly method that is suitable for large-scale batch regeneration due to its simple procedure and short reaction time.
## 5 Concluding remarks
In this review, we discussed the advancements in the field of CPs/MOFs that can afford various meaningful solutions for the treatment and control of VOCs. The properties of CPs/MOFs applicable to the control of VOCs are generally characterized by stable framework, ultra-high porosity, high internal surface area, pore volume, wide range of thermal and chemical stabilities, selectivity, feasibility of building/designing crystal structures, exceptionally high specific surface area, tunable porosity, non-toxicity, luminescence, and chemical functionality. As such, CPs/MOFs represent a potent tool that may be able to replace traditional techniques that have typically relied on common materials such as zeolites, activated carbon, organic dyes, and many other types of nanomaterials. In this respect, a number of MOFs have been tested intensively for their treatment capacity against various hazardous VOCs with the advancement of knowledge about their structural integrity. Likewise, many MOFs have also been recommended as superior sorbent materials to selectively capture low molecular weight VOCs in ambient air (e.g., capture of formaldehyde for analytical purposes). The capacity of MOFs in such
applications is indeed attractive because such requirements cannot generally be met by traditional materials (e.g., Tenax). The improvements achieved in catalytic degradation with MOFs are also impressive in many respects. Therefore, MOF technology is set to become one of the most attractive and efficient options for realizing practical control of VOCs on a wide scale.
Much effort has been directed toward overcoming the general drawbacks of MOFs in air quality management (such as high regeneration cost and reduction in structural integrity upon sorption of VOCs). Although many challenges remain in this emerging field, the common drawbacks of MOFs can be resolved or reduced by a variety of approaches (e.g., careful tuning of their structure). In addition, biological components based on MOFs or bio-inspired CPs/MOFs are also regarded as a meaningful alternative. As such, it may be worth comparing MOFs with the successful history of other materials like zeolites. While commercial success of MOFs is still pending, the reported number/ type of CPs/MOFs (about 20,000) is approximately 100 times larger than that of zeolites (218), and this wide variety of structures has been achieved in less than a quarter of the time. In addition, ongoing CPs/MOFs research requires collaborative efforts from a range of engineers and scientists to overcome their limitations including gaps in device miniaturization. Hopefully, researchers will continue to seek improved methods to reduce such gaps by investigating further options (e.g., device fabrication) to extend these materials to other potential applications.
